From a1425bb444a8de05c4ae25c212f14a309bc531c1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hibreak <hibreak@localhost>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 20:28:05 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] e-ink: sleep screen: update notification icons immediately
 without a full refresh

Previously, EinkSleepScreen only refreshed notification icons on the
once-a-minute doze time tick and on onPulsingChanged(). Any notification
that did not trigger an ambient pulse (hidden sensitive content on lock
screen, pulse disabled, grouped/updated/dismissed notifications, prox
near, or a second notification during a pulse) waited up to a full
minute to show up, and EinkDozeScreenOff kept the display off between
minute ticks. Even when a notification did pulse, DozeTriggers ran a
proximity check and kept the display ON for several seconds behind the
opaque sleep screen.

- Hook NotifPipeline in EinkSleepScreen (via
  NotificationStackScrollLayoutController): listen to
  OnAfterRenderListListener (already filtered by KeyguardCoordinator for
  lock-screen visibility) and detect changes to the displayed icon row
  (package + small Icon, covering standalone, grouped, and bundled
  notifications) without reloading drawables or waking the display when
  nothing changed.
- When the icon row changes while dozing, call
  EinkDozeScreenOff.requestUpdate() and invalidate(). Because the device
  stays asleep (vendor.xrz.hibreak_sleep stays 1), SurfaceFlinger keeps
  the sleep screen's Normal (DU) mode so only the changed icon pixels
  update with no full panel refresh, and the display turns back off
  after tick_ms (without shortening a longer pending window such as the
  initial sleep hold).
- Hold a brief CPU-only wakelock (debug.sysui.eink_sleep.notif_hold_ms,
  default 1000 ms, display still off) on relevant NotifCollection events
  while asleep so NotifPipelineChoreographer's 100 ms timeout and async
  row inflation finish before the SoC suspends.
- In DozeTriggers.onNotification(), suppress ambient notification pulses
  while EinkSleepScreen is covering AOD (mInAod), avoiding the 500 ms
  prox delay, redundant redraws, and 6+ seconds of display-on time.
